    "title": "WATERLINE: Birds, bees and blooms walk is set - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Staff Report ,\n16 hours ago\nCAPE HAZE — Everyone is invited onto a hike where the area's birds, bees and blooms will be the topic.\nIt takes place from 9-11 a.m., Monday, Jan. 19 at Don Pedro Island (Mainland) State Park at 8450 Placida Road, Cape Haze.\n\"This is not a well known natural area due to its cryptic location,\" Bill Dunson said in a news release. \"If people know anything about it, they would think that it is simply a mainland gateway to the currently closed island park and a convenient place to rent and launch kayaks. However, it is much more as an example of the fast-disappearing coastal habitat mixture consisting of salt marshes/mangroves and mesic and hydric pine flatwoods.\"\nThere will be a 1.5 mile trail across mangrove fringe and salt flats to the edge of Lemon Bay, he stated.\nIt is sponsored by the Peace River Audubon Society.\nA liability waiver needs to be signed, but reservations are not required. No dogs nor smoking on the hike. There is a $3 per vehicle fee to enter the park, he noted in the news release.\nFor more information, email wdunson@comcast.net.",
